# learn/acts -- the visible arc. the levels are a flat 00..N on disk; this file
# groups them into acts so the climb is felt, not just numbered. it is a DISPLAY
# layer only: it changes no ordering, unlocks nothing, and every gate ignores it.
#
# one act per line, tab-separated:  FROM  TO  NAME  INTRO
#   FROM TO  the inclusive level-number range the act covers (bare integers)
#   NAME     the act's title (lowercase, terse)
#   INTRO    one line, shown once, the first time you cross into the act
#
# ranges must be contiguous and cover every level; a level in no act simply
# gets no intro and no header, which is a hole worth seeing, not a crash.
0	3	hello	you and the machine, learning to talk. no flags yet -- just move around and look at what is there.
4	10	plumbing	small tools joined by pipes. this is the unix idea itself: many little programs, each doing one job, chained into one.
11	15	the language	the shell is a language. variables, tests, branches, loops, whole scripts -- now you write it, not just run it.
16	19	running things	a program is a thing you start, watch, stop, and permit. this is control of the machine while it is running.
20	24	the disk	where the bytes actually live: files packed and unpacked, checked for damage, sliced by the byte, and the disks under them.
25	28	the wire	the machine talks to other machines. bring up an interface, prove you can reach one, move data across, in the clear and sealed.
29	30	root	the editor that is always on every unix, and the power that acts as everyone. both reward care and punish haste.
31	34	the deep end	one last stretch: not new tools, but the tricks that make the ones you have do more than you thought they could.
